WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.749
Film Courage: How can a screenwriter create
a hero they want to follow?

00:00:02.749 --> 00:00:07.350
Wendy Kram, owner of Wendy’s LA4hire: I
keep saying the same thing but for me you

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:17.300
start with a character with a really strong
emotional want and I feel like we take any

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.960
successful film critically acclaimed film,
it’s always a character that has that strong

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:29.140
emotional want whether it’s in…I had mentioned
with THE WRESTLER that my upbringing I have

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:38.800
nothing in common with a wrestler.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:47.510
MILLION DOLLAR BABY, I don’t have anything
in common with a girl who wants to box, but

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.980
boy did she have a want.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.700
And I don’t mean to say that in a…I’m
trying to say the opposite of that so I’m

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:01.740
not trying to make a judgment or be snobby,
I’m saying a great film has a character

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:08.610
with a strong emotional want that no matter
what our upbringing, our race, we can identify

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.830
and relate to that character.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:18.120
The protagonist in Barry Jenkins film MOONLIGHT,
I don’t know what it’s like to grow up

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:27.340
as a young black gay man in Miami but we can
all relate to feeling like an outsider at

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:37.770
some point in our lives, wanting to be accepted,
having very extraordinarily difficult situations

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:44.510
and there was such beauty in the way this
character was able to persevere and overcome

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:51.100
and then it was told structurally and in a
very interesting manner and again there’s

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:56.280
an example where that didn’t follow what
a lot of people would say is the classic Syd

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.040
Field or Bob McKee structure.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:06.530
It was almost like three, not quite Faulkner
but there was a novelistic approach to it.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:16.840
I think you can break structure when you understand
it to begin with and you’re hitting everything

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.340
else really right.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:25.620
So writing authentic characters, compelling
dialogue, compelling situations and it is

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:26.620
well-structured.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:36.750
But I do feel it’s like tapping into that
thing that we as humans want and can identify

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:42.170
with and what are universal flaws that a character
may have.

